          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-ad55b92cc889a120\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"Birkin directly avows as true that he has loved Gerald. The perfect construction presents love as a state extending through some prior interval, while “don’t forget” turns the avowal into a bitter reminder or reproach. It does not itself undertake a future commitment, and the bitter delivery supplies no independent evidence of expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Gerald immediately questions whether Birkin truly loved him, creating uncertainty about sincerity or self-knowledge rather than about the utterance’s predominantly truth-conditional force. The elliptical scope of “as well as Gudrun” is somewhat ambiguous but does not alter the core classification.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Birkin directly addresses Gerald: “I’ve loved you, as well as Gudrun, don’t forget.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Birkin interrupts Gerald’s entranced account of his destructive relationship with Gudrun by bitterly reminding him of Birkin’s own love. The utterance asserts a neglected emotional fact and implicitly reproaches Gerald for overlooking their bond. Gerald’s icy response—“Have you? Or do you think you have?”—challenges the avowal’s truth or Birkin’s self-understanding, but does not convert it into a performative undertaking or expressive reflex.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I’ve loved you, as well as Gudrun, don’t forget,” said Birkin bitterly.\",\"supports\":\"The explicit perfect-form avowal strongly supports truth-conditional force; “don’t forget” frames it as a reminder and reproach, while “bitterly” describes delivery without independently establishing E.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Gerald replies, “Have you? Or do you think you have?” with “icy scepticism.”\",\"supports\":\"Gerald treats Birkin’s words as a claim about Birkin’s loving state whose truth or self-knowledge can be challenged, reinforcing T.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, Gerald speaks at length about Gudrun and his extreme, destructive experience with her.\",\"supports\":\"Birkin’s avowal functions contextually as a pointed reminder that his love for Gerald has been ignored amid Gerald’s fixation on Gudrun.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The supplied form analysis identifies an affirmative perfect construction.\",\"supports\":\"The grammatical form is consistent with an avowal that presents prior or continuing love as true, rather than a promise or reflexive ejaculation.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Bitterness, reproach, relational history, and Gerald’s scepticism are adequately represented as context; they create no residual core-force pressure requiring E, P, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
